  "body": "## What changed\n\n- `camcraft_sim::chuck::ring`: the bore (inner) polygon is now **inscribed** in the true bore circle (vertices on the circle, flats inside it by r(1 - cos(pi/64)), about 0.12 % of r). Outer faces are unchanged: flats on the true circle, vertices outside (circumscribed).\n- The module comment claimed the bore was conservative while the code drew it the unsafe way round; it now says what the code does and why.\n- `crates/sim/tests/lathe_chuck.rs`: the envelope is pinned to the tessellation allowance. New helper `ring_envelope` probes, at every polygon vertex and every flat middle (128 angles), steel 1e-3 mm inside each true outer face and 1e-3 mm outside each true bore wall, and air 1e-3 mm past each face's allowance. The jaw tip, gripping face and side are probed at 1e-3 mm too. The old probes stood 0.5-1 mm off the rim and jaw tip and never touched the bore wall.\n\n## Which direction is safe, and why\n\nThe collision solid is the steel; a bore is the absence of steel. A bore polygon whose flats touch the circle (circumscribed, what PR 1469 shipped) puts its vertices *outside* the bore, so the true steel between r and r/cos(pi/64) near every vertex was missing from the solid: a tool could pass there unflagged. Inscribed, the drawn bore is smaller than the true one: the solid can only claim extra steel, never drop real steel. Net rule now: every drawn face is on or outside the real steel.\n\nCost accepted (recorded in `docs/decisions/1470-chuck-bore-inscribed.md`): the drawn steel stands up to that sag into the bore. The part/stock-versus-fixture clash check is a warning only and counts past 0.05 mm, so it stays quiet for bores up to about 83 mm.\n\n## Existing test changes\n\n- `crates/app/tests/builtin_accessory.rs` `every_kind_builds_closed_shells_of_its_dimensions`: its expected chuck volume used the circumscribed polygon area for the bore; it now uses the inscribed area (n sin cos r^2). The solid's volume goes up by that sliver (more steel); bounds are unchanged (outer faces untouched).\n- `crates/sim/tests/lathe_chuck.rs`: probes tightened as above (each old steel/air probe is replaced by a stricter one at the same feature). No golden or snapshot changes.\n\n## How tested\n\nFailing first: with only the test change, `three_jaw_envelope_is_pinned` and `collet_envelope_is_pinned` failed with \"steel just outside the bore wall at 0.0000001 rad\" (the bore vertex). With the fix:\n- `cargo xtask gate quick --base origin/main`: sim lib + `sim:lathe_chuck` (6 passed), `app:builtin_accessory` - green.\n- Also `app:lathe_chuck` (3) and `app:mcp_accessory` (2) - green.\n- `cargo clippy -p camcraft-sim --all-targets -D warnings`, `cargo clippy -p camcraft-app --test builtin_accessory -D warnings` - clean.\n\nPinned by `three_jaw_envelope_is_pinned`, `collet_envelope_is_pinned` (sim) and `every_kind_builds_closed_shells_of_its_dimensions` (app).\n\n## Not exercised\n\n- The rest of the app suites (batch tier) and the release heavy lanes.\n- Pixel snapshots: a chuck render would differ by a sub-pixel sliver at most; not run, not regenerated.\n- The `fixture_clash` warning on a large (>83 mm) bore holding a part exactly at the bore: reasoned, not tested.\n- The GUI.\n\nChangelog: Lathe chuck collision solids are drawn with their bores inscribed, so the solid is never smaller than the real chuck anywhere (#1470)\nEffort: high (no escalation)\n\nFixes #1470\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
